Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level concurrency skills: non-temporal stores, false sharing avoidance, NUMA awareness, and correct lock-free sequencing.

Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers
- Non-temporal stores
- Cache-line padding to prevent false sharing
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, CAS
- NUMA pinning and microbenchmarking

Code Example
```cpp
// Slot and ring sketch (conceptual)
typedef struct alignas(256) Slot {
  uint64_t seq;
  uint8_t payload[256];
} Slot;

typedef struct {
  Slot *slots;
  _Atomic uint32_t head, tail;
} Ring;

bool ring_push(Ring *r, const void *src){
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &r->slots[h & (N-1)];
  uint64_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&s->seq, memory_order_acquire);
  if (seq != h) return false; // full
  // non-temporal payload write path placeholder
  memcpy(s->payload, src, 256);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_store_explicit(&r->head, h+1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, memory ordering, and practical reuse of barriers without locks.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic fetch_add and compare_exchange
- Memory_order_acquire/release or relaxed with correct ordering
- Sense-reversing barrier for reuse
- Spin-wait costs and NUMA locality

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { atomic_int count; atomic_int sense; int N; } barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int N){
  atomic_init(&b->count, 0);
  atomic_init(&b->sense, 0);
  b->N = N;
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, int *local_sense){
  int my_sense = !*local_sense;
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) + 1 == b->N){
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_xor_explicit(&b->sense, 1, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_acquire) != *local_sense){
      // spin
    }
  }
  *local_sense = my_sense;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic N? 
- How to avoid energy waste on long waits?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of cache-like structures, address translation, and replacement policies at low level. 

## Key Concepts
- TLB structure and page number tagging
- Linear probing and wraparound
- LRU replacement and correctness in a small cache
- Interaction with two-level page tables during misses

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint32_t tag; uint32_t pa; uint8_t valid; uint8_t lru; } tlb_entry;
static tlb_entry tlb[256];
void tlb_init() { for (int i=0;i<256;i++) tlb[i].valid=0; }
bool tlb_lookup(uint32_t va, uint32_t* pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid) continue; if(e->tag==page){ *pa = e->pa; e->lru = 0; return true; } e->lru++; } return false; }
void tlb_insert(uint32_t va, uint32_t pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; int miss_pos = -1; uint8_t oldest = 0; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid){ miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; break; } if(e->lru > oldest){ oldest = e->lru; miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; } }
 tlb[miss_pos].tag = page; tlb[miss_pos].pa = pa; tlb[miss_pos].valid = 1; tlb[miss_pos].lru = 0; }
```

